  m_Script: {fileID: 11500000, guid: 147873241f4f7964690daffb230f2761, type: 3}
  m_Name: Level2_TheInvestigation
  m_EditorClassIdentifier: 
  levelId: level_2
  levelNumber: 2
  displayName: The Investigation
  sceneName: Room2_InvestigationOffice
  description: Flagged messages arrive in the investigation office. Inspect the sender,
    context and requests before you decide.
  previewImage: {fileID: 21300000, guid: 505a3627d9fec30428c0950dace725d0, type: 3}
  briefingTitle: 'LEVEL 2: THE INVESTIGATION'
  briefingText: 'Your investigation continues. This room has six questions: four
    case questions with four answer choices and two messages or web pages to judge
    as SCAM or LEGITIMATE. They arrive in a different order on every attempt, and
    the answer choices are shuffled too, so read each one carefully instead of remembering
    positions.'
  caseNotes:
  - While browsing, a full-screen red page appeared and started beeping.
  - 'One minute before this email arrived, you clicked "Forgot password" on NovaCloud
    yourself. Trusted domain (case files): novacloud.example'
  objectives:
  - Answer all six questions in this room.
  - Read the whole situation before you choose.
  - Score 550 or more to unlock the next room.
  messages:
  - id: E07
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Multiple Choice
    body: A raffle booth at the mall asks you to fill in a form to join. <b>Which
      of these is the biggest privacy warning sign?</b>
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: The Data Privacy Act's principles include <b>transparency</b> (tell
      people why data is collected), <b>legitimate purpose</b> and <b>proportionality</b>
      (collect only what is needed). A raffle needs a way to contact you, not your
      ID and birthdate, and an ID photo plus a birthdate can be used for identity
      theft. With no notice, you can't know what happens to your data.
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - They ask for a photo of your ID and your birthdate just to join, and there
      is no privacy notice explaining why
    - The raffle is free to join
    - The staff wear company shirts
    - The draw is next week
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: An ID photo for a raffle? Too much. Ask <b>why they need it</b> and
      look for a privacy notice.
    curriculumRef: "Social and Professional Issues (BIT2105) \u2014 Week 15: Data
      Privacy Act (RA 10173)"
  - id: E08
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Multiple Choice
    body: <b>Which of your accounts deserves the strongest protection, because a
      scammer who controls it can take over many of your other accounts?</b>
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: Most services send password resets to your email. Whoever controls
      that inbox can reset the passwords of your other accounts, including social
      media, e-wallets and school accounts. That makes it your most valuable information
      asset, so it needs a unique password and MFA first.
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Your online game account
    - Your main email account, which receives password-reset links
    - Your music streaming account
    - An old forum account you no longer use
    correctChoice: 1
    feedback: Your <b>email</b> is the master key. Every 'Forgot password' link goes
      there.
    curriculumRef: "Information Assurance in Security 1 (BIT1101) \u2014 Week 9:
      Information Assets and Asset Management (\"evaluate their value and protection
      requirements\")"
  - id: E09
    channel: 2
    sender: Unknown web page
    senderDetail: 
    subject: SYSTEM SECURITY ALERT
    body: Your computer is INFECTED and LOCKED. Call Certified Support at 1-800-555-0199
      now. DO NOT restart your computer.
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: While browsing, a full-screen red page appeared and started
      beeping.
    explanation: This is a tech-support scam. Real security warnings and updates
      come from your operating system's own settings and security app, not from a
      web page with a phone number. The "support" agent will ask for remote access
      or payment. Close the tab. If it won't close, end the browser with Task Manager.
    trigger: 2
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices: []
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: Scam. Real alerts come from your <b>system settings</b>, never from
      a web page asking you to call.
    curriculumRef: "Computer Security (BIT1225) \u2014 Operating System Security
      (\"patch management, and security configuration\")"
  - id: E10
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: What Should You Do?
    body: "You paid \u20B11,500 to an online seller through PayLite. The seller then
      blocked you and never shipped. <b>What should you do first?</b>"
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: Evidence is what lets the platform, your e-wallet and the police
      act. Reference numbers and the seller's account details can help trace the
      money. Deleting the chat destroys proof. Public threats can backfire on you
      legally. "Pay more to get a refund" is a second scam.
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - Delete the chat so you can move on
    - Post the seller's photo and threaten them publicly
    - Send another payment because they say it "unlocks" your refund
    - Save screenshots of the chat, the seller's profile link and the payment receipt
      with its reference number, then report to PayLite and the authorities
    correctChoice: 3
    feedback: 'Save the evidence first: chat, profile and <b>reference number</b>.
      Then report it.'
    curriculumRef: "Cyber Forensics (BIT2226) \u2014 Digital Evidence and Evidence
      Collection (\"collection, preservation, and documentation\")"
  - id: M01
    channel: 1
    sender: NovaCloud
    senderDetail: no-reply@novacloud.example
    subject: Password reset request
    body: We received a request to reset your password. This link expires in 15 minutes.
      If you didn't request this, you can ignore this email.
    link: "https://novacloud.example/reset?t=8f3k\u2026"
    correctVerdict: 1
    scenarioContext: 'One minute before this email arrived, you clicked "Forgot password"
      on NovaCloud yourself. Trusted domain (case files): novacloud.example'
    explanation: You started this action yourself a minute ago. The sender and link
      domain match the trusted domain exactly. The email doesn't ask for your password
      or any personal details, and it tells you it's safe to ignore if you didn't
      ask for it. A reset email that proves you own the inbox is how authentication
      is supposed to work.
    trigger: 2
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 0
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices: []
    correctChoice: 0
    feedback: Legitimate. You asked for it, the domain matches, and it doesn't ask
      for your password.
    curriculumRef: "Information Assurance in Security 1 (BIT1101) \u2014 Weeks 7\u20138:
      Principles of Information Assurance (authentication)"
  - id: M02
    channel: 5
    sender: Case Review
    senderDetail: 
    subject: Identify the Threat
    body: 'A message links to: <b>https://examplebank.example.account-verify.example/login</b>.
      <b>Which is true?</b>'
    link: 
    correctVerdict: 0
    scenarioContext: 
    explanation: A domain is read <b>from the right</b>, ending just before the first
      single "/". Here that is account-verify.example, and everything to its left
      is a subdomain the attacker chose. This is not typosquatting, because nothing
      is misspelled. HTTPS only encrypts the connection (see M03).
    trigger: 0
    readingSeconds: 0
    stationId: 
    requestedAction: 
    linkNote: 
    clues: []
    inspectables: []
    evidence: []
    consequence:
      kind: 4
      alertTitle: 
      alertBody: 
      overlookedClue: 
      followUp: 1
      recheckDetails: []
    choices:
    - It's ExampleBank's site because "examplebank" appears first
    - It's safe because it uses HTTPS
    - 'It''s a subdomain trick: the real domain is account-verify.example'
    - It's a misspelled (typosquatted) bank domain
    correctChoice: 2
    feedback: 'Read the domain <b>right to left</b>: account-verify.example. The
      bank''s name is just a decoy subdomain.'
    curriculumRef: "Computer Security (BIT1225) \u2014 Web and Application Security"
